    "narrative_summary": "The dominant narrative is that diplomatic talks — principally Iran-Oman discussions and signals of a possible U.S.-Iran understanding — over reopening and controlling transit through the Strait of Hormuz are driving market and security dynamics across the Gulf. Progress or setbacks in those talks, together with parallel maritime attacks (notably Houthi strikes), create immediate feedback loops that move oil prices, equity markets, shipping traffic and insurance risk, while legislative and military postures reshape bargaining power and operational feasibility. This is an open system where diplomatic signals, kinetic incidents, and market expectations continuously interact and produce rapid reversals in prices and regional behavior.",
    "scope": "Negotiations and proposed agreements over Hormuz transit (Iran, Oman, U.S. positions), security incidents affecting Gulf and Red Sea shipping (Houthi attacks), market responses (oil prices, stocks, inventories), maritime industry reactions (shipping traffic, feasibility concerns, insurance), and related political/legislative moves in the region.",
